window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.1","title":"Random Variables and Distributions","goal":"Build the probability distribution (PMF) of the sum of two dice as a table, find its mean and variance from the PMF, and check them by simulation.","question":"A board game player rolls two fair dice and moves forward by their total. She wants to know the whole picture: how likely is each total from 2 to 12, how likely is a total of 4 or less, and what is the average total and how spread out is it? Can we build the probability distribution as a table and then check it by simulation?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The PMF as a table","text":"We count how many of the 36 equally likely pairs give each sum, then divide by 36. A pandas table keeps the values, the counts and the probabilities side by side.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom itertools import product\n\nsums = [a + b for a, b in product(range(1, 7), repeat=2)]\npmf = pd.Series(sums).value_counts().sort_index() / 36\n\ntable = pd.DataFrame({'ways': (pmf * 36).astype(int), 'P(X = x)': pmf.round(4)})\ntable.index.name = 'sum x'\nprint(table.to_string())","explain":["Load NumPy for numbers.","Load pandas to hold the table.","Load product to list all the dice pairs.","Make the total of every one of the 36 equally likely pairs.","Count how often each total occurs, sort the totals, and divide by 36. This is the probability mass function (PMF).","Put the ways to get each total and its probability side by side in one table.","Name the row labels 'sum x' so the table is easy to read.","Print the table in full."],"meaning":"The table gives 1 way to get a 2, 2 ways for a 3, rising to 6 ways for a 7 (probability 0.1667) and then falling back to 1 way for a 12. The middle totals are the most likely and the extremes the rarest.","out":"       ways  P(X = x)\nsum x                \n2         1    0.0278\n3         2    0.0556\n4         3    0.0833\n5         4    0.1111\n6         5    0.1389\n7         6    0.1667\n8         5    0.1389\n9         4    0.1111\n10        3    0.0833\n11        2    0.0556\n12        1    0.0278","figs":[]},{"title":"Check it is a valid PMF and add the CDF","text":"A valid PMF has probabilities between 0 and 1 that add to exactly 1. The running total (cumsum) is the CDF, which answers 'at most' questions at once.","code":"print('total probability:', round(pmf.sum(), 10))\n\ncdf = pmf.cumsum()\nprint(f'P(X <= 4)  = {cdf[4]:.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X >= 10) = {1 - cdf[9]:.4f}')","explain":["Add up all the probabilities. A valid PMF must total exactly 1.","Running total of the probabilities. This is the cumulative distribution function (CDF).","Read the CDF at 4: the chance the total is at most 4.","For 10 or more, take 1 minus the CDF at 9, because 'at least 10' is everything except 9 or less."],"meaning":"The probabilities add to 1.0, so this is a valid distribution. P(X <= 4) = 0.1667 and P(X >= 10) = 0.1667. They match because the distribution is symmetric around 7.","out":"total probability: 1.0\nP(X <= 4)  = 0.1667\nP(X >= 10) = 0.1667","figs":[]},{"title":"Mean and variance from the PMF","text":"The mean is the sum of x times P(x). The variance is the sum of (x minus the mean) squared times P(x). These describe the whole population of possible rolls, so there is no n - 1 here.","code":"x = pmf.index.to_numpy()\np = pmf.to_numpy()\n\nmean = (x * p).sum()\nvar = ((x - mean) ** 2 * p).sum()\n\nprint(f'mean     = {mean:.4f}')\nprint(f'variance = {var:.4f}')\nprint(f'sd       = {var ** 0.5:.4f}')","explain":["Pull out the possible totals as an array.","Pull out their probabilities as an array.","The mean is the sum of each total times its probability.","The variance is the sum of squared gaps from the mean, each weighted by its probability.","Print the mean.","Print the variance.","Print the standard deviation, the square root of the variance, back in the original units."],"meaning":"The mean total is 7.0000, the variance is 5.8333 and the standard deviation is 2.4152. A typical roll lands within about 2.4 of 7. We divide by probabilities, not by n - 1, because this is the whole population of possible rolls.","out":"mean     = 7.0000\nvariance = 5.8333\nsd       = 2.4152","figs":[]},{"title":"Check by simulation","text":"Roll two dice 100,000 times with a seeded generator. The simulated mean and variance should sit close to the exact ones. ddof=0 matches the population formula above.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nrolls = rng.integers(1, 7, size=100_000) + rng.integers(1, 7, size=100_000)\n\nprint(f'simulated mean     = {rolls.mean():.4f}')\nprint(f'simulated variance = {rolls.var(ddof=0):.4f}')\nprint(f'simulated P(X = 7) = {(rolls == 7).mean():.4f}  (exact {pmf[7]:.4f})')","explain":["Create a seeded generator so the simulation repeats.","Roll two dice 100,000 times and add them.","Print the simulated mean, which should be close to 7.","Print the simulated variance with ddof=0, the same population formula as above.","Print the simulated chance of a 7 next to the exact one."],"meaning":"The simulation gave a mean of 7.0055, a variance of 5.7999 and P(X = 7) = 0.1688, against the exact 7, 5.8333 and 0.1667. They are close but not identical because simulations have a little random error.","out":"simulated mean     = 7.0055\nsimulated variance = 5.7999\nsimulated P(X = 7) = 0.1688  (exact 0.1667)","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"s <- as.vector(outer(1:6, 1:6, \"+\"))\npmf <- table(s) / 36\nx <- as.numeric(names(pmf))\nround(pmf, 4)\nmu <- sum(x * pmf)\nmu\nsum((x - mu)^2 * pmf)\nsqrt(sum((x - mu)^2 * pmf))\n\nset.seed(42)\nrolls <- sample(6, 1e5, TRUE) + sample(6, 1e5, TRUE)\nc(mean(rolls), mean((rolls - mean(rolls))^2))","explain":["List the 36 sums with outer(), which adds every die 1 value to every die 2 value.","Count each sum with table() and divide by 36 to get the PMF.","Get the sums themselves as numbers.","Print the PMF rounded to four decimals.","The mean: sum of x times P(x).","Print the mean.","Variance: weighted squared gaps from the mean.","Standard deviation: the square root.","Fix the seed.","Simulate 100,000 rolls of two dice.","Report the simulated mean and the simulated population variance."],"expect":"mean 7; variance 5.8333; sd 2.4152; P(X = 7) = 0.1667; the simulated mean and variance are close to 7 and 5.83","read":"The printed PMF has 0.1667 for a sum of 7. Then come the mean 7, the variance 5.8333 and the standard deviation 2.4152. The simulated pair at the end should be close to 7 and 5.83, not identical.","note":"These are the population formulas, matching Python. R's var() would divide by n - 1, so for the simulation we square the gaps ourselves. Simulated values are close, not identical."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Frequencies (Statistics: Mean, Variance)","syntax":"INPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP #a = 1 TO 6.\nLOOP #b = 1 TO 6.\nCOMPUTE total = #a + #b.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nFREQUENCIES VARIABLES=total\n  /STATISTICS=MEAN VARIANCE STDDEV.","explain":["Start a program that builds the data.","Loop over the first die.","Loop over the second die.","Add the two dice to get the total.","Write a row for this pair.","Close the inner loop.","Close the outer loop.","No more rows.","End the program. We now have 36 rows.","Ask for a frequency table of the totals.","Also request the mean, variance and standard deviation."],"read":"In the Frequencies table, the Percent column is the PMF (16.7 for a total of 7) and Cumulative Percent is the CDF. In Statistics the Mean is 7.00. SPSS reports Variance 6.000 because it divides by n - 1 = 35.","note":"The Percent column is the PMF (for example 16.7 for a sum of 7) and Cumulative Percent is the CDF. The mean is 7.00. SPSS divides by n - 1 = 35, so it reports variance 6.000; multiply by 35/36 to get the population value 5.8333."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 36\ngen total = ceil(_n/6) + mod(_n - 1, 6) + 1\ntabulate total\nsummarize total\ndisplay r(Var) * 35/36\n\nclear\nset seed 42\nset obs 100000\ngen rolls = ceil(6*runiform()) + ceil(6*runiform())\nsummarize rolls","explain":["Clear memory.","Make 36 empty rows, one per pair of dice.","Create the total: the first die comes from the row number and the second repeats 1 to 6.","Show a frequency table of the totals; Percent is the PMF and Cum. is the CDF.","Get the mean and standard deviation. Stata divides by n - 1.","Convert the variance to the population version by multiplying by 35/36.","Clear the data for the simulation.","Fix the seed.","Make 100,000 empty rows.","Roll two dice per row and add them.","Summarize the simulated totals."],"read":"In the tabulate output, read the Percent column as the PMF (16.67 for 7) and Cum. as the CDF. summarize shows Mean 7, and the display line prints 5.8333, the population variance. The simulation's mean is near 7 but not identical to Python's.","note":"tabulate gives the PMF as the Percent column and Cum. as the CDF. summarize uses n - 1, so r(Var) is 6; times 35/36 gives the population variance 5.8333. The simulation is close to 7 but not identical to Python's."},"conclusion":"The total of two dice has a mean of 7, a variance of 5.8333 and an SD of 2.4152, and totals near 7 are the most likely (a 7 has probability 0.1667, a 2 or a 12 only 0.0278). Totals of 4 or less and of 10 or more each have a probability of 0.1667. The caution: the simulation matched only approximately, and these formulas assume the dice are fair and independent.","practice":{"scenario":"A shop records how many items a customer buys. After a long study the manager believes the chances are as listed below. She wants to know the average basket size, how variable it is, and how likely a customer is to buy 3 or more items.","data":"Items bought: x = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] with probabilities p = [0.10, 0.30, 0.35, 0.15, 0.10]. For the simulation use the seed 11 and 100,000 customers.","tasks":["Check that the probabilities add up to 1.","Find the mean, variance and standard deviation of the number of items.","Find P(X >= 3).","Simulate 100,000 customers and compare the mean and variance."],"hint":"The mean is the sum of x times p, and the variance is the sum of (x - mean) squared times p. For the simulation use rng.choice(x, size=100_000, p=p).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\n\nx = np.array([0, 1, 2, 3, 4])\np = np.array([0.10, 0.30, 0.35, 0.15, 0.10])\nprint('total probability:', round(p.sum(), 10))\n\nmean = (x * p).sum()\nvar = ((x - mean) ** 2 * p).sum()\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.3f}, variance = {var:.4f}, sd = {var ** 0.5:.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X >= 3) = {p[x >= 3].sum():.2f}')\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(11)\nsim = rng.choice(x, size=100_000, p=p)\nprint(f'simulated mean = {sim.mean():.3f}, variance = {sim.var():.4f}')","answer":"The probabilities add to 1.0. The mean is 1.850 items, the variance 1.2275 and the SD 1.1079, so the typical basket is within about 1.1 items of 1.85. P(X >= 3) = 0.15 + 0.10 = 0.25, so one customer in four buys 3 or more. The simulation gave a mean of 1.850 and a variance of 1.2262, matching the exact values closely.","out":"total probability: 1.0\nmean = 1.850, variance = 1.2275, sd = 1.1079\nP(X >= 3) = 0.25\nsimulated mean = 1.850, variance = 1.2262","figs":[]}}});
